<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1128181" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02232936/The-Fellowship-Of-The-Ring-Book-Cover-by-JRR-Tolkien_1-480.jpg" alt width="480" height="739" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02232936/The-Fellowship-Of-The-Ring-Book-Cover-by-JRR-Tolkien_1-480.jpg 480w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02232936/The-Fellowship-Of-The-Ring-Book-Cover-by-JRR-Tolkien_1-480-234x360.jpg 234w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02232936/The-Fellowship-Of-The-Ring-Book-Cover-by-JRR-Tolkien_1-480-156x240.jpg 156w" sizes="(max-width: 480px) 100vw, 480px"></p>
<p>In some rare cases, adaptations and interpretations of a literary work can surpass the source. Despite hundreds of valiant efforts on the part of fans, filmmakers, game/toy designers, and <a href="https://www.containsmoderateperil.com/the-lord-of-the-rings-mindseye-radio-adaptation-1979/">radio producers</a>, this has never been true of the fully-realized fantasy world in J.R.R. Tolkien’s <a href="https://amzn.to/Yq6m7U"><i>The Hobbit</i></a>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<a href="https://amzn.to/Yq6oMV"><i>The Lord of the Rings</i></a> trilogy (not that it’s ever been anyone’s intent). As we noted in a previous post, Tolkien’s fictional world is so intricate, its sources so vast and varied, that Corey Olsen, “<a href="https://tolkienprofessor.com/">The Tolkien Professor</a>,” has made it his entire life’s work to open that world up to students and curious readers, such as with his <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2013/02/download_eight_free_lectures_on_ithe_hobbiti_by_the_tolkien_professor_corey_olsen.html">eight-part lecture series on&nbsp;<em>The Hobbit</em></a><i>.</i></p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1128182" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02233048/The-Two-Towers-Book-Cover-by-JRR-Tolkien_1-480.jpg" alt width="480" height="694" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02233048/The-Two-Towers-Book-Cover-by-JRR-Tolkien_1-480.jpg 480w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02233048/The-Two-Towers-Book-Cover-by-JRR-Tolkien_1-480-249x360.jpg 249w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02233048/The-Two-Towers-Book-Cover-by-JRR-Tolkien_1-480-166x240.jpg 166w" sizes="(max-width: 480px) 100vw, 480px"></p>
<p>One might also add illustrators to the list of Tolkien interpreters above who have—in the almost ninety years since <i>The Hobbit’s</i> publication and more than seventy years since the first appearance of <i>The Lord of the Rings</i> trilogy—done their best to visualize Tolkien’s world. But perhaps no one did so better than the master himself. Long known as a visual artist as well as a literary one, Tolkien left behind over 100 illustrations for <i>The Hobbit</i>, <a href="https://www.guardian.co.uk/books/2011/oct/24/tolkien-hobbit-drawings-published">one of which adorns 2011’s HarperCollins 75th&nbsp;anniversary edition of the book</a>. He also created these original cover designs for each book in <i>The</i> <i>Lord of the Rings</i> trilogy.</p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1128183" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02233305/The-Return-Of-The-King-Book-Cover-by-JRR-Tolkien_1-480.jpg" alt width="480" height="697" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02233305/The-Return-Of-The-King-Book-Cover-by-JRR-Tolkien_1-480.jpg 480w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02233305/The-Return-Of-The-King-Book-Cover-by-JRR-Tolkien_1-480-248x360.jpg 248w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02233305/The-Return-Of-The-King-Book-Cover-by-JRR-Tolkien_1-480-165x240.jpg 165w" sizes="(max-width: 480px) 100vw, 480px"></p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1128184" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02233632/rzaoifnt6sxc1.jpeg" alt width="970" height="1214" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02233632/rzaoifnt6sxc1.jpeg 970w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02233632/rzaoifnt6sxc1-288x360.jpeg 288w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02233632/rzaoifnt6sxc1-818x1024.jpeg 818w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02233632/rzaoifnt6sxc1-192x240.jpeg 192w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/02233632/rzaoifnt6sxc1-768x961.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 970px) 100vw, 970px"></p>
<p>In the <a href="https://www.tolkienbooks.net/php/lotr.php">long and complex publication history</a> of Tolkien’s most famous of works, it’s unclear if these designs ever made it onto books published during his lifetime, but the sigil in the center of <i>The Fellowship of the Ring</i> design (above), with its ominous eye of Sauron surrounded by elvish runes and topped by the one ring, did grace the <a href="https://www.tolkienbooks.net/php/gallery.php?subsection=21">elegant, minimalist covers of the first edition of the trilogy</a>. Tolkien’s artwork received a thorough treatment in a 1995 <a href="https://www.tolkienlibrary.com/art/artistillustr/description.htm">monograph <i>J.R.R. Tolkien Artist &amp; Illustrator</i></a>, which covers over 60 years of Tolkien’s life as an artist, and the magic of flickr brings us <a href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/mnoel/3577696151/in/photostream/">this compendium</a> of Tolkien illustrations.</p>
